  <!-- ============ PART III — PROOFS (Q11-Q12) ============ -->

  <question type="essay">
    <name><text>MB11 - Proof by contradiction: nonzero rational times irrational</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>[Proof — Contradiction]</strong> Prove by contradiction that the product of a non-zero rational number and an irrational number is irrational.</p>
<p><em>Hint:</em> let \(r \ne 0\) be rational and \(x\) irrational, and suppose \(r x\) is rational.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Model proof.</strong> Let \(r\) be a non-zero rational and \(x\) irrational. Suppose, for contradiction, that \(p = r x\) is rational. Since \(r \ne 0\), we may write \(x = p / r\). The quotient of two rationals (with non-zero denominator) is rational, so \(x\) would be rational — contradicting that \(x\) is irrational. Therefore \(r x\) is irrational. \( \blacksquare \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0</penalty>
