  <question type="essay">
    <name><text>MB12 - Proof by contraposition: 5n+3 odd implies n even</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>[Proof — Contraposition]</strong> Let \( n \in \mathbb{Z} \). Prove that <em>if \( 5n + 3 \) is odd then \( n \) is even</em>, using contraposition.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Model proof.</strong> The contrapositive is: "if \(n\) is odd then \(5n+3\) is even." Assume \(n\) is odd, so \(n = 2k+1\). Then \( 5n+3 = 5(2k+1)+3 = 10k+8 = 2(5k+4) \), which is even. Hence the contrapositive holds, so the original statement is true. \( \blacksquare \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
